Chapter: OtaBoot
  cites Foreword chapter OtaUpdate
  cites Foreword chapter Board
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter Hkdf
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The selector is deliberately small, because it runs in EVERY boot, not
 only after an update: read one word, maybe hash one bank, choose a bank.
 No policy engine, no trust lattice, no CDX parse. Gate B already decided
 whether this image is allowed to run; the selector only decides whether
 what is in the bank is what Gate B blessed, and whether it has had too
 many chances.

 The record lives in flash and the flag is ONE aligned word, which is the
 design's atomicity requirement and not a detail: the device must wake
 having either fully accepted the candidate or never heard of it. The
 digest and length are written first and the flag last, in its own
 unlock-program-lock cycle, so a power loss between them leaves a digest
 nobody points at and a device that boots the firmware it already
 trusted.

 We say:

Section: The Record

 A device that has never been updated has no record. Erased NOR flash
 reads as all-ones and a zeroed page reads as all-zeros, and neither is a
 boot record, so the word carries a marker. Anything else is the factory
 state: boot the primary, no candidate. That is also the honest answer
 after a power loss during the very first write.

  boot-marker : Integer = #B0

  boot-attempt-limit : Integer = 3

  BootRecord = record {
   br-primary : BootSlot,
   br-candidate : Maybe BootSlot,
   br-attempts : Integer between 0 and 255
  }

  boot-factory-record : BootRecord
  boot-factory-record = BootRecord {
   br-primary = SlotA,
   br-candidate = None,
   br-attempts = 0
  }

  slot-bit : BootSlot -> Integer
  slot-bit (s) =
   when s
    is SlotA -> 0
    is SlotB -> 1

  bit-slot : Integer -> BootSlot
  bit-slot (b) = if b == 0 then SlotA else SlotB

 Bit 0 primary, bit 1 candidate-present, bit 2 candidate slot, bits 8-15
 attempts, bits 24-31 the marker. One word, one store.

  boot-flag-word : BootRecord -> Integer
  boot-flag-word (r) =
   let p = slot-bit (r.br-primary)
   in let c = when r.br-candidate
       is None -> 0
       is Just (s) -> bit-or 2 (bit-shl (slot-bit s) 2)
   in bit-or (bit-or (bit-or p c) (bit-shl (r.br-attempts) 8)) (bit-shl boot-marker 24)

  boot-record-of-word : Integer -> BootRecord
  boot-record-of-word (w) =
   if bit-and (bit-shru w 24) 255 /= boot-marker then boot-factory-record
   else BootRecord {
    br-primary = bit-slot (bit-and w 1),
    br-candidate = if bit-and w 2 == 0 then None else Just (bit-slot (bit-and (bit-shru w 2) 1)),
    br-attempts = __narrow (bit-and (bit-shru w 8) 255)
   }

Section: The Transitions

  ota-mark-candidate : BootRecord, BootSlot -> BootRecord
  ota-mark-candidate (r) (c) = BootRecord {
   br-primary = r.br-primary,
   br-candidate = Just c,
   br-attempts = 0
  }

 An attempt is counted BEFORE the candidate runs, never after. A boot
 that hangs, faults, or browns out never reaches an after -- counting
 there would give a hanging image infinite retries, which is the failure
 the limit exists to stop.

  ota-boot-attempt : BootRecord -> BootRecord
  ota-boot-attempt (r) = BootRecord {
   br-primary = r.br-primary,
   br-candidate = r.br-candidate,
   br-attempts = __narrow (if r.br-attempts >= 255 then 255 else r.br-attempts + 1)
  }

 Rollback forgets the candidate and keeps the primary. The candidate
 never committed, so the committed sequence never advanced and this is
 not an anti-rollback violation -- it is the device declining to adopt an
 image it could not run.

  ota-rollback : BootRecord -> BootRecord
  ota-rollback (r) = BootRecord {
   br-primary = r.br-primary,
   br-candidate = None,
   br-attempts = 0
  }

 The commit is step 7, and it is explicit by design: the new firmware
 says it is healthy, and nothing says it on the firmware's behalf. An
 implicit commit -- adopting an image because it reached the selector --
 would defeat the watchdog window, because an image that boots and then
 wedges is exactly the image the window exists to catch. No commit means
 the next boot counts another attempt and eventually rolls back.

  ota-commit : BootRecord -> BootRecord
  ota-commit (r) =
   when r.br-candidate
    is None -> r
    is Just (c) -> BootRecord {
     br-primary = c,
     br-candidate = None,
     br-attempts = 0
    }

  boot-select : BootRecord, Boolean -> BootSlot
  boot-select (r) (candidate-ok) =
   when r.br-candidate
    is None -> r.br-primary
    is Just (c) ->
      if candidate-ok == False then r.br-primary
      else if r.br-attempts > boot-attempt-limit then r.br-primary
      else c

  boot-give-up : BootRecord, Boolean -> Boolean
  boot-give-up (r) (candidate-ok) =
   when r.br-candidate
    is None -> False
    is Just (c) -> (candidate-ok == False) | (r.br-attempts > boot-attempt-limit)

Section: The Flash Half

 Reading the record is a memory read; writing it goes through the Flash
 effect, so a caller without [Flash] can look at the boot record and
 cannot move it.

  boot-flag-offset : Integer = 0
  boot-digest-offset : Integer = 8
  boot-len-offset : Integer = 48

  boot-read-word : Integer -> Integer
  boot-read-word (addr) =
   let b0 = peek-byte addr 0
   in let b1 = bit-shl (peek-byte addr 1) 8
   in let b2 = bit-shl (peek-byte addr 2) 16
   in let b3 = bit-shl (peek-byte addr 3) 24
   in bit-or (bit-or b0 b1) (bit-or b2 b3)

  boot-load : Integer -> BootRecord
  boot-load (rec-addr) = boot-record-of-word (boot-read-word (rec-addr + boot-flag-offset))

  boot-read-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  boot-read-bytes (addr) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else boot-read-bytes addr (i + 1) len (list-push acc (peek-byte addr i))

  boot-expected-digest : Integer -> List Integer
  boot-expected-digest (rec-addr) = boot-read-bytes (rec-addr + boot-digest-offset) 0 32 []

  boot-candidate-length : Integer -> Integer
  boot-candidate-length (rec-addr) = boot-read-word (rec-addr + boot-len-offset)

  boot-store : linear Board, Integer, Integer, BootRecord -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] (linear Board, Integer)
  boot-store (brd) (bank) (rec-addr) (r) = act
   ob <- flash-open-bank brd bank
   when ob
    is (brd1, b0) -> act
      b1 <- flash-write-word b0 (rec-addr + boot-flag-offset) (boot-flag-word r)
      s <- flash-seal-bank b1
      board-pair brd1 s
    end
  end

  boot-mark : linear Board, Integer, Integer, BootRecord, BootSlot, List Integer, Integer -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] (linear Board, Integer)
  boot-mark (brd) (bank) (rec-addr) (r) (cand) (digest) (len) = act
   ob <- flash-open-bank brd bank
   when ob
    is (brd1, b0) -> act
      b1 <- flash-write-page b0 (rec-addr + boot-digest-offset) digest
      b2 <- flash-write-word b1 (rec-addr + boot-len-offset) len
      f <- flash-seal-bank b2
      boot-store brd1 bank rec-addr (ota-mark-candidate r cand)
    end
  end

 Step 6's re-check: hash the bank and compare against the digest Gate A
 recorded. Constant heap -- sha256-buf reads the address, the image is
 never a List. This is the hash half of Gate A. It is NOT the signature
 half: the signature was checked at Gate A and again by Gate B's verifier
 before this bank was ever marked, and re-parsing a CDX header to recheck
 it would put a parser in every boot path, which is the one thing the
 design says this code must not become. What this catches is the bank
 changing after Gate B blessed it -- a partial write, a bad cell, a
 flipped bit -- which is what a boot-time re-check is for.

  boot-verify-candidate : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  boot-verify-candidate (rec-addr) (slot-addr) =
   let len = boot-candidate-length rec-addr
   in if len <= 0 then False
   else ota-hash-eq (hkdf-words-to-bytes (sha256-buf slot-addr 0 len)) (boot-expected-digest rec-addr) 0 32

Section: The Selector

 The whole of step 6, in the order that survives a power loss: count the
 attempt and persist it BEFORE hashing anything, so an image that hangs
 during verification has still spent a chance.

  slot-address : BootSlot, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  slot-address (s) (a-addr) (b-addr) =
   when s
    is SlotA -> a-addr
    is SlotB -> b-addr

  boot-check : BootRecord, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  boot-check (counted) (rec-addr) (slot-addr) =
   if counted.br-attempts > boot-attempt-limit then False
   else boot-verify-candidate rec-addr slot-addr

  boot-give-up-store : linear Board, Integer, Integer, BootRecord, Boolean -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] (linear Board, Integer)
  boot-give-up-store (brd) (bank) (rec-addr) (r) (ok) =
   if boot-give-up r ok then boot-store brd bank rec-addr (ota-rollback r)
   else board-pair brd 0

  boot-run-candidate : linear Board, Integer, Integer, Integer, BootRecord, BootSlot -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] (linear Board, BootSlot)
  boot-run-candidate (brd) (bank) (rec-addr) (slot-addr) (r) (c) =
   let counted = ota-boot-attempt r
   in act
    w <- boot-store brd bank rec-addr counted
    when w
     is (brd1, wr) ->
       let ok = boot-check counted rec-addr slot-addr
       in act
        g <- boot-give-up-store brd1 bank rec-addr counted ok
        when g
         is (brd2, gr) -> board-pair brd2 (boot-select counted ok)
        end
       end
   end

  boot-run : linear Board, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] (linear Board, BootSlot)
  boot-run (brd) (bank) (rec-addr) (slot-a-addr) (slot-b-addr) =
   let r = boot-load rec-addr
   in when r.br-candidate
    is None -> board-pair brd (r.br-primary)
    is Just (c) -> boot-run-candidate brd bank rec-addr (slot-address c slot-a-addr slot-b-addr) r c

  boot-commit : linear Board, Integer, Integer -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] (linear Board, Integer)
  boot-commit (brd) (bank) (rec-addr) =
   let r = boot-load rec-addr
   in boot-store brd bank rec-addr (ota-commit r)
